UNIT multiply
KIND FUNCTIONAL
ORACLE-CLASS deterministic
SIG multiply(multiplier?, multiplicand?) -> product
    # default/main export: `module.exports = multiply`. Emitter MUST `export default <fn>`.
    # Both params optional (arity 2). Any JS value accepted for each.
DEPENDS-ON none   # zero runtime dependencies; self-contained (bundled custom lodash build)

# ============================================================================
# OVERVIEW
# ============================================================================
# `multiply` is `createMathOperation(operator=(a,b)=>a*b, defaultValue=1)`.
# It is a PURE function: no side effects, no ambient reads, no carried data,
# no seam. ORACLE-CLASS deterministic.
#
# It is built on three private helpers reproduced below (baseToNumber,
# baseToString, isSymbol). These are NOT a custom radix-parsing toNumber:
# this 4.9.0 modular build uses `baseToNumber` (essentially unary `+`, with a
# symbol short-circuit) and `baseToString` (essentially `String(value)`, with a
# symbol short-circuit and a `-0` special case). All binary/octal/hex/whitespace
# string parsing is therefore performed by the HOST JS engine's native ToNumber
# (invoked by the `*` operator on strings, or by unary `+`), NOT by lodash.

# ============================================================================
# PRIVATE HELPERS (in-contract logic — reproduce exactly)
# ============================================================================

# isSymbol(value):
#   returns true iff:  typeof value === 'symbol'
#                   OR (value is object-like (truthy && typeof==='object')
#                       AND Object.prototype.toString.call(value) === '[object Symbol]')
#   (the second clause catches boxed Symbol objects)

# baseToNumber(value):   # "coerce to number"
#   1. if typeof value === 'number'  -> return value           # identity, incl. NaN/±Infinity/-0
#   2. if isSymbol(value)            -> return NaN
#   3. otherwise                     -> return +value          # native unary plus / ToNumber
#      NOTE: +bigint THROWS TypeError ("Cannot convert a BigInt value to a number").
#            This throw is therefore propagated by multiply (see BRANCH N3 below).
#      Native +value rules that matter here:
#        +'  5  ' = 5 ; +'' = 0 ; +'  ' = 0 ; +'0x10' = 16 ; +'0b11' = 3 ;
#        +'0o17' = 15 ; +'1e3' = 1000 ; +'abc' = NaN ; +'Infinity' = Infinity ;
#        +true = 1 ; +false = 0 ; +null = 0 ; +undefined = NaN ;
#        +[] = 0 ; +[5] = 5 ; +[1,2] = NaN ; +{} = NaN ;
#        +object -> ToPrimitive(value,'number') -> valueOf() then toString().

# baseToString(value):   # "coerce to string"
#   1. if typeof value === 'string'  -> return value
#   2. if isSymbol(value)            -> return value.toString() (e.g. 'Symbol()');
#                                       if the runtime has no Symbol, return ''
#   3. otherwise:
#        result = (value + '')                                  # ToPrimitive default hint (valueOf-first)
#        if result === '0' AND (1 / value) === -Infinity        # i.e. value is the number -0
#             -> return '-0'
#        else -> return result
#      NOTE: `(value + '')` uses the DEFAULT ToPrimitive hint, so for plain
#            objects it invokes valueOf() BEFORE toString() (same primitive
#            source as baseToNumber's `+`). Demonstrated equivalence:
#            {valueOf:()=>7, toString:()=>'99'} stringifies to '7', not '99'.
#      Examples: null -> 'null' ; undefined -> 'undefined' ; true -> 'true' ;
#                123 -> '123' ; -0 -> '-0' ; [5] -> '5' ; [1,2] -> '1,2' ;
#                {} -> '[object Object]' ; Infinity -> 'Infinity' ; NaN -> 'NaN'.

# ============================================================================
# BEHAVIOR — multiply(value, other)   (param names in source: value, other;
#                                       public doc names: multiplier, multiplicand)
# ============================================================================
# Evaluate the branches IN ORDER. `===` / `!==` against `undefined` is strict
# (only the literal `undefined` value triggers default handling; `null` does NOT).
#
# BRANCH D0  (both undefined):
#   if value === undefined AND other === undefined:
#       return defaultValue   # == 1  (NOT 0). multiply()=1, multiply(undefined,undefined)=1.
#
# Set result := value if value !== undefined, else result stays undefined.
#
# BRANCH P1  (other undefined, value defined)  -- i.e. one-arg / second-undefined:
#   Reached when other === undefined but value !== undefined (D0 already excluded
#   both-undefined). The `if (other !== undefined)` block below is skipped, so:
#       return result   # == value, RETURNED AS-IS, NO COERCION.
#   This is identity passthrough preserving type & reference:
#       multiply(7)        -> 7            multiply(7, undefined)        -> 7
#       multiply('abc')    -> 'abc' (string, not NaN)
#       multiply(null)     -> null  (typeof object)   multiply(NaN)     -> NaN
#       multiply(-0)       -> -0           multiply(Infinity)           -> Infinity
#       multiply(3n)       -> 3n (bigint, NO throw — coercion never runs)
#       multiply(obj)      -> the SAME object reference (identity preserved)
#       multiply(fn)       -> the SAME function reference
#
# BRANCH P2  (value undefined, other defined):
#   Reached when value === undefined but other !== undefined. Then result is
#   still undefined when we enter the `if (other !== undefined)` block, and the
#   first sub-check `if (result === undefined) return other;` fires:
#       return other   # RETURNED AS-IS, NO COERCION (mirror of P1).
#       multiply(undefined, 7)     -> 7        multiply(undefined, 0)    -> 0
#       multiply(undefined, 'abc') -> 'abc'    multiply(undefined, null) -> null
#       multiply(undefined, NaN)   -> NaN      multiply(undefined, obj)  -> SAME ref
#       multiply(undefined, 3n)    -> 3n (no throw)
#
# --- Below here: BOTH value and other are defined (!== undefined). The product
#     is computed. The coercion path depends on whether EITHER is a string. ---
#
# BRANCH S  (string path):  if typeof value === 'string' OR typeof other === 'string':
#       a = baseToString(value)
#       b = baseToString(other)
#       return operator(a, b)   # == a * b  (native `*` on two STRINGS; JS coerces
#                                 each via native ToNumber, then multiplies)
#   Consequences (native string-* ToNumber):
#       multiply('6','4') -> 24      multiply('6',4) -> 24    multiply(6,'4') -> 24
#       multiply('abc',4) -> NaN     multiply('',4)  -> 0     multiply('  ',2) -> 0
#       multiply('  5  ',2) -> 10    multiply('0x10',2) -> 32 multiply('0b11',2) -> 6
#       multiply('0o17',2) -> 30     multiply('1e3',2) -> 2000 multiply('3.14',2) -> 6.28
#       multiply('Infinity',2) -> Infinity
#       multiply(Infinity,'2') -> Infinity   # baseToString(Infinity)='Infinity'
#       multiply(-0,'5') -> -0       # baseToString(-0)='-0'; '-0' * '5' = -0
#       multiply('5',-0) -> -0       multiply('-0','5') -> -0
#       multiply(null,'2') -> NaN    # baseToString(null)='null'; 'null'*'2'=NaN
#       multiply(true,'2') -> NaN    # 'true'*'2'=NaN
#       multiply([5],'2') -> 10      multiply([1,2],'2') -> NaN  # '1,2'*'2'=NaN
#       multiply(Symbol(),'4') -> NaN # baseToString(Symbol())='Symbol()'; *'4'=NaN (NO throw)
#       multiply(3n,'4') -> 12       # baseToString(3n)='3'; '3'*'4'=12 (NO throw — string path)
#       multiply(obj,'3') with valueOf->7 -> 21  # baseToString(obj)='7' via (obj+'')
#
# BRANCH N  (number path):  else (NEITHER value nor other is a string):
#       a = baseToNumber(value)
#       b = baseToNumber(other)
#       return operator(a, b)   # == a * b
#   Sub-cases of baseToNumber feeding `*`:
#     N1  plain numbers: ordinary IEEE-754 multiply with full propagation:
#         multiply(6,4)=24  multiply(3,-2)=-6  multiply(2.5,2)=5
#         multiply(NaN,4)=NaN  multiply(Infinity,4)=Infinity  multiply(-Infinity,4)=-Infinity
#         multiply(Infinity,0)=NaN
#         signed-zero: multiply(-0,5)=-0  multiply(-0,-5)=0  multiply(0,-5)=-0  multiply(-1,0)=-0
#     N2  non-number, non-string, non-symbol, non-bigint -> +value:
#         multiply(true,4)=4  multiply(false,4)=0  multiply(true,true)=1
#         multiply(null,4)=0  multiply(4,null)=0
#         multiply({},4)=NaN  multiply([],4)=0  multiply([5],4)=20  multiply([1,2],4)=NaN
#         multiply({valueOf:()=>10},4)=40   multiply(new Date(1000),2)=2000
#         multiply(fn,4)=NaN  (where fn is a function; +fn=NaN)
#     N3  symbol -> baseToNumber returns NaN (NO throw on number path):
#         multiply(Symbol(),4)=NaN  multiply(4,Symbol())=NaN  multiply(Symbol(),Symbol())=NaN
#     N4  bigint -> baseToNumber does `+bigint` which THROWS TypeError:
#         multiply(3n,4n)  THROWS TypeError  multiply(3n,4) THROWS  multiply(2,3n) THROWS
#         (A bigint reaches the number path only when the OTHER arg is not a
#          string and not undefined. multiply(3n) and multiply(3n,'4') do NOT throw.)
#
# RETURN TYPE: number for all coercion paths (S, N); EXCEPT the identity
#   passthroughs P1/P2 and the default D0, which return the original value /
#   defaultValue with its original type (e.g. multiply('abc')->'abc' is a string,
#   multiply(3n)->3n is a bigint, multiply()->1 is a number).

# ============================================================================
ORACLE value -> oracle.json
#   Harness calls multiply(...args), records the return (or {__throw} for the
#   bigint TypeError path). Deterministic, value-mode. No seam, no carried data.
#   NOTE for the verifier: -0 vs 0, NaN, ±Infinity, and bigint passthrough must
#   be compared with Object.is / type-aware equality (not ===), since the codec
#   round-trips -0, NaN, ±Infinity and bigint.

# ============================================================================
SCOPE
#   IN-CONTRACT LOGIC (reproduce exactly): the entire branch tree above
#     (D0, P1, P2, S, N1-N4) plus helpers isSymbol, baseToNumber, baseToString.
#   CARRIED DATA: none.
#   OUT-OF-CONTRACT (stateful/side APIs): none — pure function.
#
#   OUT-OF-ENVELOPE (behavior IS specified above, but NOT emitted by genInputs
#   because the input codec cannot faithfully round-trip the value, so the blind
#   re-emitter reconstructs these from the spec, not from a stored input):
#     - Symbol args: typeof 'symbol' does not survive JSON codec. SPEC: on number
#       path -> NaN (N3); on string path (other is string) -> baseToString gives
#       'Symbol()' -> NaN. Never throws.
#     - Function args: not codec-round-trippable. SPEC: P1/P2 passthrough returns
#       the same fn reference; on number path +fn = NaN.
#     - Boxed primitives (new Number(5), new String('5')): object identity does
#       not survive codec. SPEC: typeof is 'object' (NOT 'string'/'number'), so
#       they take the NUMBER path -> +boxed via valueOf: new Number(5)*2=10,
#       new String('5')*2=10, new String('abc')*2=NaN; passthrough preserves ref.
#     - Plain objects/arrays with custom valueOf/toString: a {} or [] DOES round-
#       trip structurally, but a custom valueOf/toString method does NOT survive
#       JSON. genInputs emits only plain {} / [] / numeric-array literals whose
#       valueOf/toString are the defaults; the custom-method behavior is specified
#       (S/N branches via (v+'') default hint) for the blind re-emitter.
#     - bigint args DO round-trip the codec; genInputs MAY emit them. The throwing
#       combos (N4) are gradable via the {__throw} record; the non-throwing combos
#       (3n alone -> 3n; 3n with a string -> coerced) are gradable as values.

# ============================================================================
PROVENANCE
#   package: lodash.multiply@4.9.0  (MIT)
#   repo: lodash/lodash  (modularized custom build: `lodash modularize exports="npm"`)
#   source file: node_modules/lodash.multiply/index.js
#     createMathOperation @ lines 89-113; baseToNumber @ 51-59;
#     baseToString @ 69-79; isSymbol @ 160-163; multiply (=createMathOperation
#     of `(a,b)=>a*b` with defaultValue 1) @ 180-184; module.exports @ 184.
